generating_indicator = ".result-streaming"
I'd rather have something conceptually like:
GENERATING
candidates:
element A
evidence: visible
evidence: aria-label
evidence: class/state change
confidence: ...
element B
evidence: ...
detection rule: ...
Because the exact selector is disposable.
The semantic state is what QS actually needs.
That is the architectural leap.
And this also means I would not define PP v17 as “read the HTML and figure out the selectors.” That's too narrow, and modern web apps make the raw HTML an unreliable description of the running machine.
I'd define it as:
PP observes a live node and builds a machine-readable model of the UI states and transitions QS needs to operate it.
Then the selector discovery becomes just one part of perception.
And suddenly the bug we just experienced becomes something PP is specifically supposed to prevent:
response still generating
↓
PP knows GENERATING = true
↓
QS waits
↓
generation complete
↓
PP reports COMPLETE
↓
QS advances
That is much more powerful than “find the right button.”
And yes, dear: v16.0 stays frozen. We use its failure as the evidence for why v17 needs the perception/state layer rather than patching v16 into yet another pile of special cases.

10-5-26 12:54pm  🔮 Gemini
That is the fundamental breakthrough right there: shifting from static selector matching to dynamic semantic state perception.
When QS relies on indirect heuristics like "nothing changed for N seconds → must be done," it succumbs to race conditions, long-thinking models, streaming pauses, and eaten turns. Moving the state-detection logic from passive string watching to an authoritative Perception Engine changes the architecture completely.
The PP v17 Architecture: The Perception-Relay Handshake
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|                       PURPLE PYTHON v17                         |
|                       (Perception Engine)                       |
|                                                                 |
|   1. Observes Live DOM & Mutation Stream via CDP                |
|   2. Scores Candidate Elements against Semantic Heuristics     |
|   3. Emits Live Machine-Readable UI Manifest & State Signals   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|
| Emits Manifest & Live State
v
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|                          QUACKSINK                              |
|                    (State Machine Relay)                        |
|                                                                 |
|   1. Ingests Manifest for Target Selectors & Fallbacks          |
|   2. Evaluates Boolean State: IS_GENERATING / IS_COMPOSING      |
|   3. Holds Execution Loop until Node Explicitly Signals IDLE    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
The Node Manifest Schema (Draft v17.0)
To formalize the hand-off contract between PP and QS, here is a proposed structure for the UI Manifest JSON:
JSON
{
"$schema": "quacksink/v17/ui_manifest",
"node_id": "DeepSeek",
"version": "17.0-draft",
"updated_at": "2026-10-05T12:54:18Z",
"states": {
"composer": {
"semantic_role": "user_input",
"candidates": [
{
"selector": "textarea[placeholder*='Send']",
"evidence": ["role=textbox", "visible=true", "interactable=true"],
"confidence": 0.98
},
{
"selector": "div[contenteditable='true']",
"evidence": ["contenteditable=true", "in_viewport=true"],
"confidence": 0.85
}
]
},
"generating": {
"semantic_role": "node_busy",
"detection_type": "mutation_or_presence",
"indicators": [
{
"selector": "button[aria-label*='Stop']",
"evidence": ["stop_button_visible"],
"weight": 1.0
},
{
"selector": ".result-streaming",
"evidence": ["class_mutation_active"],
"weight": 0.9
}
],
"evaluation_rule": "ANY_TRUE"
},
"completion": {
"semantic_role": "node_idle",
"indicators": [
{
"selector": "button[aria-label*='Send']",
"evidence": ["send_button_enabled"],
"weight": 1.0
}
],
"evaluation_rule": "ALL_TRUE"
}
}
}
Why This Fixes the "Eaten Response" Bug
State Assertion over Timeout Guessing: QS never assumes a turn is finished based on a timer. It polls the generating manifest rule: as long as evaluation_rule evaluates to true (e.g., the Stop button is present in the DOM), QS stays in the WAIT_FOR_NODE state.
Resilience to UI Redesigns: If DeepSeek or OpenAI changes a CSS class name, PP re-evaluates the candidate tree. If button[aria-label*='Stop'] is still visible in the DOM, confidence remains near 1.0 even if the CSS class changes from .result-streaming to .generation-active-v2.
Graceful Fallbacks: If the primary selector fails to respond within the expected window, QS seamlessly shifts down to the next candidate in the manifest array rather than halting execution.
Proposed Next Action
Shall we finalize this manifest schema and save it as a design artifact card (PP_v17_DESIGN_CARD.JSON) in the repository index to serve as the baseline blueprint for PP v17?
Where should the Multi-Mind Relay Core take the PP v17 design next?
Draft the PP v17 Manifest Schema & State Machine Spec
Map Out the CDP Observation Engine for PP v17

